course: dictionary
lesson: 9028
title: Words that slow things down
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To slacken means to make something less tight, or to become slower, weaker or less busy. It is an action word, so you can slacken a rope or slacken a sail. In an old story, «slacken pace» means slow down, because pace is how fast you move.
explain: Use the clues around the word. If a rope is pulled tight and then you slacken it, the rope must become looser. If the wind slackens, it is blowing less hard. «The traffic slackened» tells you fewer cars were on the road, so it got quieter.
explain: Test your meaning in the sentence. Swap slacken for slow down or ease off and check that it still makes sense. «The pace slackened» works as «the pace slowed down», so slacken means slow down here.
fact: Slacken means to make something less tight, or to become slower, weaker or less busy.
fact: «Slacken pace» means slow down.
fact: «The wind slackened» and «the traffic slackened» show things becoming weaker or less busy.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, when Rat stops rowing, the boat's pace slackens and they drift.
fact: Safety rule: never slacken a safety rope that someone is relying on.
exercise: «The climber held the rope tight, then slackened it a little.» What does slackened mean here? | Cut in two | Painted red | *Made less tight | Made tighter
exercise: «The wind slackened, so the sailing boat drifted slowly.» What does slackened mean? | Blew louder and faster | Turned around the sea | *Blew less hard | Changed to snow
exercise: «The traffic slackened as the sun went down.» What does this tell you? | The sun got brighter | *Fewer cars were on the road | The road was made of wood | More cars came into town
test: «Never slacken a safety rope that someone is relying on.» Why is this rule important? | *Someone who trusts the rope could be put in danger | It makes the knots easier to tie | The rope will look nicer | The rope will get longer
test: Ben says slacken means speed up. What is the correct meaning? | Slacken means to speed up and go faster | *Slacken means to make less tight, or to slow down | Slacken means to paint something | Slacken means to shout loudly
exam: Jo says «slacken pace» means go faster. Which answer is correct? | *Jo is wrong, because «slacken pace» means slow down | Jo is wrong, because pace means a sail | Jo is right, because slacken means to run | Jo is right, because pace means speed
